.TH DOTFILE-MANAGER 1 "September 2026" "dotfile-manager 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dotfile-manager \- safely discover, inventory, compare, back up, and restore dotfiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dotfile-manager
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dotfile-manager
is a Linux command-line utility that keeps a managed copy of chosen
configuration files (dotfiles) in a store directory, defaulting to
.IR $HOME/dotfiles ,
and safely synchronizes them with the real home directory.
.PP
It is designed around a small set of hard safety rules rather than
convenience:
it never overwrites existing files without
.B \-\-dry\-run / \-\-force
semantics; discovery never scans the whole home directory (it is bounded
to
.I $HOME
at depth 1 plus
.I $HOME/.config
one level deeper); credential-shaped paths
(\c
.IR ~/.ssh ", " ~/.gnupg ", " ~/.aws/credentials ", " ~/.config/gcloud ,
password stores, cookie databases, tokens) are classified sensitive and
are never auto-added, read, or printed; management mode (copy vs.
symlink) is never changed implicitly; and
.B remove
never deletes the actual file, only the management record.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B discover
Read-only scan for candidate dotfiles. Takes no positional arguments and
rejects
.B \-\-force
with a usage error (exit 2).
.TP
.BI "list " "[\-\-snapshots]"
List currently managed entries, or (with
.BR \-\-snapshots )
list available snapshots.
.TP
.BI "add " path " [options]"
Add
.I path
(relative to
.IR $HOME )
to the managed set. Copies the current content into the store and
records the path, mode, and any tags in the configuration file. Never
converts the home file into a symlink, even when
.B \-\-mode symlink
is given \(em that conversion only happens via an explicit
.B "apply \-\-force"
later.
.TP
.BI "remove " path " [\-\-purge\-store]"
Stop managing
.IR path .
Does not delete the file at
.IR path .
With
.BR \-\-purge\-store ,
also deletes the store's copy \(em but refuses on a non-empty directory
and never performs a recursive delete.
.TP
.BI "status " "[paths...]"
Show the sync status of managed entries: one of
.BR IN_SYNC ", " MODIFIED ", " MISSING_HOME ", " MISSING_STORE ,
.BR TYPE_MISMATCH ", " SYMLINK_MISMATCH ", " CONFLICT ", or " UNKNOWN .
.TP
.BI "diff " path
Show differences between the store and home versions of
.IR path .
Binary files are reported as differing without content being printed as
a textual diff.
.TP
.BI "backup " "[paths...] [\-\-snapshot]"
Copy current home content into the store for the given entries (or all
managed entries). Refuses conflicting changes unless
.B \-\-force
is given; always previewable with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
With
.BR \-\-snapshot ,
creates a timestamped snapshot of the store before syncing.
.TP
.BI "apply " "[paths...]"
Apply store content out to the home directory for the given entries (or
all managed entries), including performing the copy\(->symlink
conversion for entries in symlink mode. Creates a safety backup of any
existing differing home content before overwriting it. Refuses
conflicts unless
.B \-\-force
is given; always previewable with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.BI "restore \-\-snapshot\-id " "ID|latest " "[paths...]"
Restore home content from a previously created snapshot.
.B \-\-snapshot\-id
is required.
.TP
.B check
Validate the configuration file and all managed paths without changing
anything.
.TP
.B report
Produce a complete status and check report in one invocation.
.TP
.B version
Print version and license information.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-format " "text|json|markdown"
Select output format (default: text).
.TP
.BI "\-\-output " file
Write output to
.I file
instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Include additional detail in output.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential messages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color output.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Preview a mutating operation without changing anything. Only accepted by
commands that perform mutations.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Allow an explicitly-documented destructive or overwriting operation to
proceed. Only accepted by commands that perform mutations.
.TP
.B \-\-help ", " \-h
Show usage help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show version information.
.SH ADD OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-mode " "copy|symlink"
Management mode for the new entry (default: copy). Recorded in the
configuration; does not itself modify the home file.
.TP
.BI "\-\-store\-path " path
Override the relative path used to store the entry (default: same as
the home-relative path).
.TP
.BI "\-\-tag " name
Attach a tag to the entry. May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-sensitive
Required to add a path that dotfile-manager classifies as sensitive
(credential-shaped paths). Without this flag,
.B add
refuses such paths.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success \(em operation completed, or everything already in sync.
.TP
.B 1
Differences or non-fatal warnings were found.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Configuration error. The configuration file is malformed and is left
untouched.
.TP
.B 4
Path-safety error (traversal attempt, symlink escape, special file,
filesystem loop, or similar).
.TP
.B 5
Unresolved conflict; the operation was refused because
.B \-\-force
was not given.
.TP
.B 6
I/O failure.
.TP
.B 7
Internal error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dotfile-manager/config.json
(or
.IR ~/.config/dotfile-manager/config.json )
Configuration file listing managed entries, the store location, and
ignore patterns. Never rewritten if malformed.
.TP
.I $HOME/dotfiles
Default store directory holding managed copies of dotfile content.
.TP
.I <store>/.dotfile-manager/safety-backups/
Automatic safety backups of home content, made immediately before any
overwrite performed by
.BR apply .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Base of the bounded discovery scan and of all home-relative path
resolution.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the default configuration directory.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Overrides the default state directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Preview what could be managed:
.RS
.EX
dotfile-manager discover
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start tracking a file and inspect its status:
.RS
.EX
dotfile-manager add .bashrc
dotfile-manager status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Preview then perform pulling store content onto a new machine:
.RS
.EX
dotfile-manager apply \-\-dry\-run
dotfile-manager apply \-\-force
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Like other tools that operate on a live filesystem,
.B dotfile-manager
does not provide hard time-of-check-to-time-of-use guarantees against a
concurrently running adversarial process; it minimizes this window by
never following symlinks blindly and re-verifying immediately before
mutating, but does not claim atomicity the operating system does not
provide.
.SH BUGS
Report issues at the project's issue tracker.
